	doi = {10.1063/1.3445776},
	abstract = {Transmission electron microscopy(TEM) investigations of epitaxialgraphene,grown on on-axis and 8{\textdegree} off-axis C-terminated 4H{\textendash}SiC ( 000 1 {\textasciimacron} ) surfaces are presented. The TEM results provide evidence that the first carbon layer is separated by 3.2 {\r A} from the C-terminated SiCsurface. It was also found that thick graphene layers grown on on-axis SiC ( 000 1 {\textasciimacron} ) are loosely bound to the SiC substrate. Moreover, the structural observations reveal a certain degree of disorder between the graphene planes, which manifests itself in a rotation of the layers and in an increase in the interplanar spacing between certain carbon layers from 3.35 {\r A}, which is characteristic for graphite, up to 3.7 {\r A}. Graphenegrown on 8{\textdegree} off-axis SiC ( 000 1 {\textasciimacron} ) substrates covers the steps of SiC and as a result disorder seems to be not as pronounced as it is on the on-axis SiC ( 000 1 {\textasciimacron} ) substrate.},
